"Think back to the granary in Lesson 2. A village has hundreds of sheep, many families, and a store of grain that has to last until the next harvest. Who brought how much? Who still owes some? Memory is not enough for questions like these. In the cities of southern Mesopotamia, people began to answer them with marks pressed into small pieces of soft clay.": "b0ee4b5ea029",
"Think back to the granary in Lesson 2. A village has hundreds of sheep, many families, and a store of grain that has to last until the next harvest. Who brought how much? Who still owes some? Memory is not enough for questions like these. In the cities of southern Mesopotamia, people began to answer them with marks pressed into small pieces of soft clay. The oldest known writing comes from the city of Uruk, about 3350 to 3000 BCE. A scribe pressed the end of a cut reed into wet clay, and the tablet hardened as it dried in the hot, dry air. Most of these early tablets are accounts: grain, animals, workers, and the food paid to them. A smaller number are lists of words, probably used to train scribes. Writing here began as a tool for keeping track of things. Writing was not invented only once. Many scholars count four places where it began from scratch: Mesopotamia, Egypt, China, and Mesoamerica. In Egypt, labels from early royal tombs date from about the same time as the Uruk tablets. In China, the earliest surviving writing was carved on shells and bones more than 3,000 years ago, recording the Shang kings' questions and predictions about the future, addressed to their ancestors and spirits. In Mesoamerica, writing recorded dates in a calendar, and later the names and deeds of rulers. A picture can show a sheep or a jar of grain, but how do you draw a name, or a word like “and”? The answer was a clever trick: use a picture for its sound. In English, a drawing of an eye could stand for the word “I.” Egyptian hieroglyphs used both kinds of signs, some for meanings and some for sounds. Most Chinese characters joined a part that hints at the meaning with a part that hints at the sound. Perhaps as early as 1800 BCE, people who spoke a Semitic language took a few Egyptian signs and used each one for a single sound. Exactly where and when this happened is still argued over. This was the beginning of the alphabet, which later passed to the Phoenicians, the Greeks, and the Romans. An alphabet needs far fewer signs. But that does not make it better than other scripts. Chinese characters, Japanese kana, and many other systems serve the languages they write very well. Writing changed what people could do. A promise, a law, or a list could now outlast the person who made it. For most of its history, though, writing was the skill of a few, such as trained scribes. Most people passed on their history by telling it. In Taiwan, the long age before written records is called prehistory. The National Museum of Prehistory names Chen Di's account of 1603 as the earliest record of Taiwan's customs; older Chinese texts may mention the island, but scholars disagree.": "c58a3bc2122f",
